  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "b1",
      "function": "hook",
      "scene_type": "rv_hook",
      "spoken": "According to NGK, a spark plug fires up to nine thousand five hundred times a minute. Here is how it is built. From top to bottom, part by part.",
      "data": {
        "claim": "Up to 9,500 sparks a minute",
        "claim_lines": [
          "Up to 9,500",
          "sparks a minute"
        ],
        "claim_highlight": "9,500",
        "claim_at": 0.05,
        "counters": false,
        "theta": [
          [
            0,
            0
          ],
          [
            1,
            1440
          ]
        ],
        "fade_in": false,
        "fade_out": false
      },
      "overlay": {
        "marker": "fakt",
        "marker_at": "w:NGK",
        "source": false
      },
      "source_ids": [
        "S2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b2",
      "function": "problem",
      "scene_type": "rv_plug",
      "spoken": "At the top, the terminal for the ignition lead. Below it, the insulator made of aluminium oxide, a ceramic. It keeps the high voltage in the middle apart from the metal outside. Without that barrier, the current would take a short cut instead of jumping the spark gap.",
      "data": {
        "title": "Terminal and insulator",
        "labels": [
          "terminal",
          "insulator"
        ],
        "labels_at": [
          "w:terminal",
          "w:insulator"
        ],
        "theta": [
          [
            0,
            0
          ],
          [
            1,
            360
          ]
        ],
        "fade_in": false,
        "fade_out": false
      },
      "overlay": {
        "marker": "fakt",
        "marker_at": "w:aluminium",
        "kicker": "2 · THE TOP",
        "transition": {
          "type": "blende",
          "seconds": 0.7
        }
      },
      "source_ids": [
        "S2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b3",
      "function": "explanation",
      "scene_type": "rv_plug",
      "spoken": "Inside sits a suppression resistor made of a carbon and glass compound. Outside, the metal shell with its hexagon. And according to NGK, the gasket carries heat away into the cylinder head. It damps the electrical interference the spark creates.",
      "data": {
        "title": "Resistor and shell",
        "labels": [
          "resistor",
          "hex",
          "gasket"
        ],
        "labels_at": [
          "w:resistor",
          "w:shell",
          "w:gasket"
        ],
        "heat_at": [
          "w:heat"
        ],
        "theta": [
          [
            0,
            0
          ],
          [
            1,
            360
          ]
        ],
        "fade_in": false,
        "fade_out": false
      },
      "overlay": {
        "marker": "fakt",
        "marker_at": "w:NGK",
        "kicker": "3 · THE MIDDLE"
      },
      "source_ids": [
        "S1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b4",
      "function": "demonstration",
      "scene_type": "rv_plug",
      "spoken": "Right at the bottom, the centre electrode. According to NGK it is normally two point six millimetres thick, and only zero point six on iridium plugs. Opposite sits the ground electrode, and the spark jumps in between. The distance between them is the electrode gap.",
      "data": {
        "title": "The electrodes",
        "labels": [
          "center",
          "ground"
        ],
        "labels_at": [
          "w:centre",
          "w:ground"
        ],
        "sparks_at": [
          "w:spark"
        ],
        "theta": [
          [
            0,
            0
          ],
          [
            1,
            360
          ]
        ],
        "fade_in": false,
        "fade_out": false
      },
      "overlay": {
        "marker": "fakt",
        "marker_at": "w:iridium",
        "kicker": "4 · THE BOTTOM"
      },
      "source_ids": [
        "S1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b5",
      "function": "insight",
      "scene_type": "rv_board",
      "spoken": "The first NGK plug was made in nineteen thirty-six, and iridium plugs have existed since nineteen ninety-seven. Depending on engine speed, NGK says a plug fires between three hundred and fifty and nine thousand five hundred times a minute. That is up to about a hundred and sixty sparks a second.",
      "data": {
        "title": "From 1936 to today",
        "mode": "history",
